  "prompt": "A cosine curve has amplitude $4$, midline $y = 1$, period $2\\pi$, and reaches its maximum at $x = 0$. Write its equation, starting with $y =$ (you may leave off the \"$y =$\").",
  "answerType": "expression",
  "primaryAnswer": "4cos(x) + 1",
  "alternativeAnswers": [
    "4cos(x)+1",
    "1 + 4cos(x)",
    "1+4cos(x)",
    "4cosx + 1",
    "4*cos(x)+1"
  ],
  "acceptanceMode": "normalized",
  "hint": "Build it piece by piece: $y = A\\cos(Bx) + D$.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "Build it piece by piece: $y = A\\cos(Bx) + D$."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "Amplitude gives $A = 4$; midline gives $D = 1$; a period of $2\\pi$ means $B = 1$."
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "$y = 4\\cos(x) + 1$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Amplitude $4$ means $A = 4$.",
    "Midline $y = 1$ means the vertical shift is $D = 1$.",
    "Period $2\\pi$ is the ordinary cosine period, so $B = 1$ and no horizontal squeeze is needed.",
    "Cosine already peaks at $x = 0$, so no phase shift is needed either: $y = 4\\cos(x) + 1$."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "Check it against the description",
      "steps": [
        "At $x = 0$: $4\\cos(0) + 1 = 4(1) + 1 = 5$, the maximum — and $5$ is indeed $4$ above the midline $1$.",
        "At $x = \\pi$: $4(-1) + 1 = -3$, the minimum, $4$ below the midline. Everything matches."
      ]
    }
  ],
